To turn this mock-up into a colorful website, you can use HTML and CSS to create the page's layout, and JavaScript to add interactivity and function. First, create a simple HTML structure with a container for the text and button elements. Then, include CSS to style the website elements according to desired colors, typography, and layout. Finally, use JavaScript and a joke API to replace the placeholder text with actual jokes when the button is clicked. Here's a brief example:
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>My Joke Website</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
</head>
<body>
    <div id="buttons">
        <p>Button 1</p>
        <button id="btn1">Click me</button>
    </div>
    <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

```css
#buttons {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
}

#btn1 {
    background-color: #FFC100;
}
```

```javascript
const jokeAPI = "https://api.jokeapi.dev/jokes/2023/funny";

function fetchJoke() {
    fetch(jokeAPI)
        .then((response) => response.json())
        .then((data) => {
            const joke = ` ${data.primary_category}, ${data.title}, ${data.joke}`;
            document.getElementById("btn1").innerHTML = joke;
        })
        .catch((error) => console.error("Error fetching joke:", error));
}

const btn1 = document.getElementById("btn1");
btn1.addEventListener("click", fetchJoke);
```
Remember to replace the example joke API URL with your desired joke API when implementing this code in your project.

